window.PRAC_SUBJECTS = [{"id":"static","n":"Static GK","tab":"Static GK","w":"Dances, music, festivals, monuments, books, sports, awards and firsts.","topics":[{"id":"dance","n":"Dances","qs":[{"id":"p0001","q":"Sattriya, one of the eight classical dances of India, belongs to which state?","o":["Odisha","Manipur","Assam","Tripura"],"a":2,"x":"Sattriya grew in the Vaishnava monasteries (sattras) of Assam founded by Srimanta Sankardeva in the 15th century. Sangeet Natak Akademi recognised it as a classical dance in 2000.","e":"Sattriya comes from 'sattra', the Assamese monastery — the dance is named after the building it was born in.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0002","q":"Mohiniyattam, performed mainly by women as a solo dance, is the classical dance of which state?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Kerala","Karnataka","Andhra Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Mohiniyattam is Kerala's classical dance. The name means 'dance of the enchantress' (Mohini, the female form of Vishnu); it uses gentle, swaying movements and a white-and-gold costume.","e":"Kerala has two classical dances: Kathakali (dramatic, with painted faces) and Mohiniyattam (gentle, solo).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0003","q":"Which classical dance form takes its name from a village in Andhra Pradesh?","o":["Odissi","Kathak","Bharatanatyam","Kuchipudi"],"a":3,"x":"Kuchipudi is named after Kuchipudi village in Krishna district, Andhra Pradesh. It mixes dance with spoken dialogue and is known for the tarangam, danced on the rim of a brass plate.","e":"Kuchipudi = a village name; the dancer balancing on a brass plate is its signature.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0004","q":"Chhau, inscribed on UNESCO's list of Intangible Cultural Heritage in 2010, is NOT traditionally performed in which of these states?","o":["Odisha","Kerala","Jharkhand","West Bengal"],"a":1,"x":"Chhau is a masked martial dance of Odisha (Mayurbhanj), Jharkhand (Seraikella) and West Bengal (Purulia). Kerala's classical forms are Kathakali and Mohiniyattam instead.","e":"Three Chhau styles, three states: Mayurbhanj (Odisha), Seraikella (Jharkhand), Purulia (West Bengal).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0005","q":"Rukmini Devi Arundale founded which institution for the revival of Bharatanatyam?","o":["Darpana Academy","Kadamb School of Dance","Kalakshetra","Nrityagram"],"a":2,"x":"Rukmini Devi Arundale founded Kalakshetra at Adyar, Chennai, in 1936. She brought Bharatanatyam from temple courtyards onto the modern stage.","e":"Kalakshetra = 'place of art'; Chennai, 1936, Rukmini Devi Arundale.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0006","q":"Ghoomar is a well-known folk dance of which state?","o":["Gujarat","Haryana","Punjab","Rajasthan"],"a":3,"x":"Ghoomar is a Rajasthani folk dance performed by women in flowing ghagras who move in a circle; the name comes from 'ghoomna', to twirl.","e":"Ghoomar twirls in Rajasthan; Garba circles in Gujarat; Bhangra leaps in Punjab.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"music","n":"Music and Instruments","qs":[{"id":"p0007","q":"Pandit Shivkumar Sharma was the master of which musical instrument?","o":["Sarod","Sarangi","Shehnai","Santoor"],"a":3,"x":"Shivkumar Sharma brought the santoor, a many-stringed Kashmiri folk instrument struck with wooden mallets, onto the Hindustani classical stage.","e":"Santoor = Shivkumar Sharma; shehnai = Bismillah Khan; sarod = Amjad Ali Khan.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0008","q":"Which of the following is a percussion instrument?","o":["Sitar","Mridangam","Sarangi","Bansuri"],"a":1,"x":"The mridangam is the main percussion instrument of Carnatic music. The sitar is plucked, the sarangi is bowed and the bansuri is a wind instrument.","e":"Mridangam is to Carnatic music what the tabla is to Hindustani music.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0009","q":"Tyagaraja, Muthuswami Dikshitar and Syama Sastri are together known as the trinity of which form of music?","o":["Hindustani music","Dhrupad","Carnatic music","Qawwali"],"a":2,"x":"These three 18th-19th century composers are called the Trinity of Carnatic music; between them they wrote much of its core repertoire.","e":"All three were born in Thiruvarur, Tamil Nadu — the Carnatic trinity from one town.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"festivals","n":"Fairs and Festivals","qs":[{"id":"p0010","q":"The Hornbill Festival, held every December, is celebrated in which state?","o":["Meghalaya","Mizoram","Nagaland","Arunachal Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"The Hornbill Festival is held at Kisama near Kohima, Nagaland, every December. It brings together the major Naga tribes and is called the 'Festival of Festivals'.","e":"The hornbill — the bird whose feathers appear in Naga headgear — gives Nagaland's festival its name.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0011","q":"Pongal, a four-day harvest festival, is chiefly celebrated in which state?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Kerala","Karnataka","Telangana"],"a":0,"x":"Pongal is the Tamil harvest festival held in mid-January, when freshly harvested rice is boiled in milk until it overflows — a sign of plenty.","e":"Same season, different names: Pongal (Tamil Nadu), Makar Sankranti (north), Magh Bihu (Assam), Lohri (Punjab).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0012","q":"Onam, the harvest festival associated with the legendary King Mahabali, is celebrated in which state?","o":["Goa","Kerala","Odisha","Andhra Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Onam is Kerala's ten-day harvest festival in the month of Chingam, marking the yearly homecoming of King Mahabali. It features the Vallam Kali snake-boat race and the Onasadya feast.","e":"Onam: flower carpets (pookalam), snake boats and a feast served on a banana leaf.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"art","n":"Monuments, Art and Architecture","qs":[{"id":"p0013","q":"The Khajuraho group of temples was built by which dynasty?","o":["Chalukya","Chandela","Pallava","Rashtrakuta"],"a":1,"x":"The Khajuraho temples in Madhya Pradesh were built by the Chandela rulers between about 950 and 1050 CE. They became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1986.","e":"Khajuraho = Chandelas = Madhya Pradesh, built in the nagara style.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0014","q":"The Kailasa temple at Ellora, carved out of a single rock, was built under which dynasty?","o":["Chola","Gupta","Satavahana","Rashtrakuta"],"a":3,"x":"The Kailasa temple (Cave 16) at Ellora was carved downwards out of one basalt hill during the reign of the Rashtrakuta king Krishna I in the 8th century.","e":"Ellora's Kailasa was cut from the hilltop down — the largest monolithic excavation in the world.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0015","q":"The Brihadeeswarar Temple at Thanjavur was built by which ruler?","o":["Rajendra Chola I","Rajaraja Chola I","Krishnadevaraya","Narasimhavarman I"],"a":1,"x":"Rajaraja Chola I completed the Brihadeeswarar temple at Thanjavur in 1010 CE. It is one of the 'Great Living Chola Temples' on the UNESCO list.","e":"Rajaraja built Thanjavur; his son Rajendra built Gangaikonda Cholapuram.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0016","q":"The Charminar in Hyderabad was built by which ruler?","o":["Ibrahim Adil Shah","Aurangzeb","Nizam-ul-Mulk","Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah"],"a":3,"x":"Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah built the Charminar in 1591, when Hyderabad was founded. Its name means 'four minarets'.","e":"Char (four) + minar (tower) — Quli Qutb Shah, 1591, Hyderabad.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0017","q":"The Hawa Mahal in Jaipur was built by which ruler?","o":["Sawai Pratap Singh","Sawai Jai Singh II","Man Singh I","Ram Singh II"],"a":0,"x":"Sawai Pratap Singh built the Hawa Mahal in 1799; it was designed by Lal Chand Ustad. Its many small windows let royal women watch street processions unseen.","e":"Hawa Mahal = 'palace of winds' — a five-storey screen of 953 jharokhas.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"books","n":"Books and Authors","qs":[{"id":"p0018","q":"The Rajatarangini, a chronicle of the kings of Kashmir, was written by whom?","o":["Banabhatta","Kalhana","Bilhana","Jayadeva"],"a":1,"x":"Kalhana wrote the Rajatarangini in Sanskrit in the 12th century. It is often called India's earliest work of proper historical writing.","e":"Rajatarangini = 'river of kings'; Kalhana, Kashmir, 12th century.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0019","q":"Who wrote the Harshacharita, a biography of King Harshavardhana?","o":["Kalidasa","Vishakhadatta","Banabhatta","Vishnu Sharma"],"a":2,"x":"Banabhatta was Harshavardhana's court poet and wrote both the Harshacharita and the romance Kadambari.","e":"Bana wrote the life of his own patron Harsha — a court poet writing the king's story.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0020","q":"The Ain-i-Akbari was written by whom?","o":["Abul Fazl","Badauni","Faizi","Abdul Hamid Lahori"],"a":0,"x":"Abul Fazl, one of Akbar's nine gems, wrote the Akbarnama; the Ain-i-Akbari is its third volume and records the administration, revenue and statistics of Akbar's empire.","e":"The Akbarnama has three volumes — the last one, Ain-i-Akbari, is the gazetteer.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0021","q":"'Wings of Fire' is the autobiography of which person?","o":["A. P. J. Abdul Kalam","Jawaharlal Nehru","Vikram Sarabhai","Homi Bhabha"],"a":0,"x":"'Wings of Fire', written with Arun Tiwari and published in 1999, is A. P. J. Abdul Kalam's autobiography covering his life up to 1991.","e":"Kalam's other well-known titles: 'Ignited Minds' and 'India 2020'.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0022","q":"The novel 'Godan' was written by whom?","o":["Phanishwar Nath Renu","Harivansh Rai Bachchan","Munshi Premchand","Jaishankar Prasad"],"a":2,"x":"'Godan' (1936) is Munshi Premchand's last completed novel; it follows the peasant Hori and is regarded as a classic of Hindi fiction.","e":"Godan = 'the gift of a cow' — the wish Hori never manages to fulfil.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"sports","n":"Sports","qs":[{"id":"p0023","q":"The Thomas Cup is associated with which sport?","o":["Table tennis","Lawn tennis","Badminton","Squash"],"a":2,"x":"The Thomas Cup is the world men's team championship in badminton; the women's equivalent is the Uber Cup. India won the Thomas Cup for the first time in 2022.","e":"Badminton team cups: Thomas (men), Uber (women), Sudirman (mixed).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0024","q":"Who was the first Indian to win an individual gold medal at the Olympic Games?","o":["Neeraj Chopra","Rajyavardhan Singh Rathore","Vijender Singh","Abhinav Bindra"],"a":3,"x":"Abhinav Bindra won gold in the 10 m air rifle at Beijing in 2008 — India's first individual Olympic gold. Neeraj Chopra won the second, in the javelin, in 2021.","e":"Bindra 2008 (shooting), Chopra 2021 (javelin).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0025","q":"The term 'bully' is used in which sport?","o":["Cricket","Hockey","Football","Volleyball"],"a":1,"x":"'Bully' is the hockey term for the old way of restarting play, with two opponents tapping sticks over the ball. Modern rules have replaced it in most situations.","e":"Hockey words: bully, dribble, penalty corner, short corner.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0026","q":"National Sports Day in India is observed on 29 August to mark the birth anniversary of which sportsperson?","o":["Milkha Singh","K. D. Jadhav","Major Dhyan Chand","Jaipal Singh Munda"],"a":2,"x":"National Sports Day is held on 29 August, the birthday of hockey player Major Dhyan Chand, who won Olympic golds in 1928, 1932 and 1936. The national sports awards are given on this day.","e":"Dhyan Chand was called 'The Wizard'; the Khel Ratna award is now named after him.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"misc","n":"Awards, Firsts, Days and Organisations","qs":[{"id":"p0027","q":"The headquarters of the International Court of Justice is located in which city?","o":["Geneva","Vienna","The Hague","Paris"],"a":2,"x":"The International Court of Justice, the principal judicial organ of the United Nations, sits at the Peace Palace in The Hague, Netherlands.","e":"The ICJ is the only main UN organ that does not sit in New York.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0028","q":"The headquarters of UNESCO is located in which city?","o":["Paris","Rome","Geneva","Nairobi"],"a":0,"x":"UNESCO, the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization, has its headquarters in Paris, France.","e":"Paris: UNESCO. Rome: FAO. Nairobi: UNEP. Vienna: IAEA.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0029","q":"National Science Day is observed in India on 28 February to mark the discovery of which phenomenon?","o":["Photoelectric effect","Doppler effect","Raman effect","Zeeman effect"],"a":2,"x":"National Science Day marks C. V. Raman's announcement of the Raman effect on 28 February 1928; he received the Nobel Prize in Physics for it in 1930.","e":"Raman effect = scattering of light with a change of wavelength; 28 February, Nobel 1930.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0030","q":"Who was the first woman President of India?","o":["Indira Gandhi","Sarojini Naidu","Sucheta Kripalani","Pratibha Patil"],"a":3,"x":"Pratibha Patil was President of India from 2007 to 2012 — the first woman to hold the office. Droupadi Murmu, who took office in 2022, is the second.","e":"First woman Prime Minister: Indira Gandhi. First woman President: Pratibha Patil.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0031","q":"Which is the highest civilian award of India?","o":["Padma Vibhushan","Padma Bhushan","Param Vir Chakra","Bharat Ratna"],"a":3,"x":"The Bharat Ratna, instituted in 1954, is India's highest civilian award. The Param Vir Chakra is the highest military gallantry award, not a civilian one.","e":"Civilian order: Bharat Ratna, then Padma Vibhushan, Padma Bhushan, Padma Shri.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0032","q":"Rakesh Sharma, the first Indian to travel to space, flew aboard a spacecraft of which country?","o":["United States","France","Soviet Union","Japan"],"a":2,"x":"Rakesh Sharma flew on the Soviet Soyuz T-11 mission in April 1984 and spent about eight days aboard the Salyut 7 space station.","e":"Asked by Indira Gandhi how India looked from space, he answered 'Saare Jahan Se Achcha'.","src":["Practice question"]}]}]},{"id":"hist","n":"History","tab":"History","w":"Ancient and medieval India, British India, and the freedom struggle.","topics":[{"id":"ancient","n":"Ancient India","qs":[{"id":"p0033","q":"The Harappan site of Lothal, known for its dockyard, is located in which present-day state?","o":["Rajasthan","Haryana","Punjab","Gujarat"],"a":3,"x":"Lothal, in Gujarat, has a large brick basin generally identified as a dockyard, pointing to sea trade with West Asia.","e":"Gujarat's Harappan sites: Lothal (dockyard), Dholavira (water tanks), Surkotada (horse bones).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0034","q":"The Great Bath, a large watertight tank, was found at which Harappan site?","o":["Harappa","Mohenjodaro","Kalibangan","Ropar"],"a":1,"x":"The Great Bath at Mohenjodaro is a brick tank made watertight with gypsum mortar and a layer of bitumen, with steps at each end. It is usually read as a site of ritual bathing.","e":"Mohenjodaro = Great Bath and the Great Granary; it lies on the Indus in Sindh, Pakistan.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0035","q":"Who deciphered the Brahmi script used in Ashoka's edicts?","o":["James Prinsep","John Marshall","Alexander Cunningham","William Jones"],"a":0,"x":"James Prinsep deciphered Brahmi and Kharosthi in 1837, which made it possible to read Ashoka's inscriptions and identify the king called 'Devanampiya Piyadasi' as Ashoka.","e":"Prinsep read the script; Cunningham founded the Archaeological Survey of India.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0036","q":"Gautama Buddha delivered his first sermon at which place?","o":["Bodh Gaya","Sarnath","Kushinagar","Lumbini"],"a":1,"x":"The Buddha gave his first sermon, the Dhammachakkappavattana Sutta, at the deer park in Sarnath near Varanasi. This event is called the turning of the wheel of law.","e":"Lumbini born, Bodh Gaya enlightened, Sarnath first sermon, Kushinagar died.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0037","q":"The third Buddhist Council was held during the reign of which ruler?","o":["Ashoka","Ajatashatru","Kanishka","Kalashoka"],"a":0,"x":"The third Buddhist Council met at Pataliputra under Ashoka, with Moggaliputta Tissa presiding. The fourth was held in Kashmir under Kanishka.","e":"Councils: 1st Rajagriha (Ajatashatru), 2nd Vaishali, 3rd Pataliputra (Ashoka), 4th Kashmir (Kanishka).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0038","q":"Mahavira, the 24th Tirthankara of Jainism, attained nirvana at which place?","o":["Vaishali","Pavapuri","Rajgir","Shravanabelagola"],"a":1,"x":"Mahavira died and attained nirvana at Pavapuri in present-day Bihar, around 468 BCE by the traditional reckoning.","e":"Mahavira: born at Kundagrama near Vaishali, died at Pavapuri.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0039","q":"The Allahabad Pillar inscription, which records the conquests of Samudragupta, was composed by whom?","o":["Banabhatta","Harisena","Vishakhadatta","Ravikirti"],"a":1,"x":"Harisena, Samudragupta's court poet, composed the Prayag Prashasti engraved on an older Ashokan pillar at Allahabad.","e":"Harisena praised Samudragupta, who is often called the 'Napoleon of India'.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0040","q":"The Chinese pilgrim Fa-Hien visited India during the reign of which ruler?","o":["Chandragupta Maurya","Chandragupta II","Harshavardhana","Kanishka"],"a":1,"x":"Fa-Hien travelled in India between about 399 and 414 CE, during the reign of Chandragupta II Vikramaditya. Hiuen Tsang came later, under Harshavardhana.","e":"Fa-Hien with the Guptas; Hiuen Tsang with Harsha; Itsing later still.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"medieval","n":"Medieval India","qs":[{"id":"p0041","q":"Who was the first woman ruler of the Delhi Sultanate?","o":["Chand Bibi","Nur Jahan","Rani Durgavati","Razia Sultan"],"a":3,"x":"Razia Sultan, daughter of Iltutmish, ruled Delhi from 1236 to 1240 — the only woman to sit on the throne of the Delhi Sultanate.","e":"Iltutmish chose his daughter over his sons; the nobles later removed her.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0042","q":"Which Sultan of Delhi shifted the capital from Delhi to Daulatabad?","o":["Alauddin Khilji","Muhammad bin Tughlaq","Firoz Shah Tughlaq","Balban"],"a":1,"x":"Muhammad bin Tughlaq moved the capital to Devagiri, renamed Daulatabad, around 1327 and ordered the people of Delhi to move there. The experiment failed and the court returned.","e":"Muhammad bin Tughlaq's two famous failures: the Daulatabad move and token currency.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0043","q":"The Vijayanagara Empire was founded in 1336 by which pair of brothers?","o":["Hemu and Adil Shah","Krishna and Rama Raya","Devaraya and Bukka II","Harihara and Bukka"],"a":3,"x":"Harihara I and Bukka Raya I founded Vijayanagara on the Tungabhadra in 1336, starting the Sangama dynasty. Its ruins are at Hampi in Karnataka.","e":"Vijayanagara = 'city of victory'; founded 1336, broken after Talikota in 1565.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0044","q":"In which battle was the Vijayanagara Empire decisively defeated in 1565?","o":["Battle of Raichur","Battle of Chandawar","Battle of Khanwa","Battle of Talikota"],"a":3,"x":"The combined Deccan Sultanates defeated Vijayanagara at Talikota in 1565; the city of Hampi was sacked afterwards.","e":"Talikota 1565 — after it, Hampi was left in the ruins visitors see today.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0045","q":"Who founded the Mughal Empire in India after the First Battle of Panipat?","o":["Humayun","Akbar","Babur","Sher Shah Suri"],"a":2,"x":"Babur defeated Ibrahim Lodi at the First Battle of Panipat in 1526 and founded the Mughal Empire. He used field artillery and the tulughma formation.","e":"Panipat 1526 (Babur), 1556 (Akbar v Hemu), 1761 (Abdali v Marathas).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0046","q":"Akbar abolished the jizya tax in which year?","o":["1564","1556","1582","1605"],"a":0,"x":"Akbar abolished the jizya, the tax on non-Muslims, in 1564, after ending the pilgrimage tax the previous year. Aurangzeb reimposed it in 1679.","e":"Akbar removed jizya in 1564; Aurangzeb brought it back in 1679.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0047","q":"The Battle of Haldighati in 1576 was fought between Maharana Pratap and the forces of which ruler?","o":["Akbar","Babur","Jahangir","Sher Shah Suri"],"a":0,"x":"At Haldighati in 1576, Maharana Pratap of Mewar fought Akbar's army led by Raja Man Singh of Amber. Pratap withdrew but was never captured.","e":"Haldighati 1576: Maharana Pratap and his horse Chetak against Man Singh.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0048","q":"Shivaji was formally crowned Chhatrapati in 1674 at which fort?","o":["Raigad","Sinhagad","Pratapgad","Shivneri"],"a":0,"x":"Shivaji's coronation took place at Raigad fort in June 1674, where he took the title Chhatrapati. He was born at Shivneri fort.","e":"Born at Shivneri, crowned at Raigad.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0049","q":"Who founded the Sikh religion?","o":["Guru Angad","Guru Nanak","Guru Arjan Dev","Guru Gobind Singh"],"a":1,"x":"Guru Nanak (1469-1539) founded Sikhism and was the first of the ten Gurus. Guru Gobind Singh, the tenth, founded the Khalsa in 1699.","e":"Ten Gurus: first Nanak, tenth Gobind Singh, then the Guru Granth Sahib itself.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"modern","n":"Modern India (1757–1885)","qs":[{"id":"p0050","q":"The Battle of Buxar in 1764 was fought between the English East India Company and the combined forces of which three leaders?","o":["Siraj-ud-daulah, Mir Jafar and Shah Alam II","Tipu Sultan, the Nizam and the Marathas","Mir Qasim, Shuja-ud-daulah and Shah Alam II","Mir Jafar, the Nizam and Haider Ali"],"a":2,"x":"At Buxar in 1764 the Company defeated the joint army of Mir Qasim of Bengal, Shuja-ud-daulah of Awadh and the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II. The Treaty of Allahabad in 1765 then gave the Company the Diwani of Bengal, Bihar and Odisha.","e":"Plassey won Bengal by intrigue; Buxar won it in open battle and brought the Diwani.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0051","q":"The Permanent Settlement of Bengal in 1793 was introduced by whom?","o":["Lord Wellesley","Lord Cornwallis","Warren Hastings","Lord Dalhousie"],"a":1,"x":"Lord Cornwallis introduced the Permanent Settlement in 1793, fixing the land revenue of Bengal, Bihar and Odisha for ever and making zamindars the owners of the land.","e":"Permanent Settlement = Cornwallis + zamindars; Ryotwari = Thomas Munro; Mahalwari = village bodies.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0052","q":"The practice of sati was legally abolished in 1829 during the time of which Governor-General?","o":["Lord Bentinck","Lord Dalhousie","Lord Canning","Lord Ripon"],"a":0,"x":"Lord William Bentinck banned sati by Regulation XVII of 1829, supported by Raja Ram Mohan Roy's long campaign against it.","e":"Bentinck 1829: sati banned, with Raja Ram Mohan Roy behind the reform.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0053","q":"Who is regarded as the father of the Indian Renaissance and founded the Brahmo Samaj?","o":["Dayanand Saraswati","Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar","Raja Ram Mohan Roy","Swami Vivekananda"],"a":2,"x":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy founded the Brahmo Sabha in 1828, which became the Brahmo Samaj. He campaigned against sati and for women's education.","e":"Brahmo Samaj: Ram Mohan Roy, 1828. Arya Samaj: Dayanand Saraswati, 1875.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0054","q":"The Arya Samaj was founded in 1875 by whom?","o":["Swami Dayanand Saraswati","Swami Vivekananda","Annie Besant","Keshab Chandra Sen"],"a":0,"x":"Swami Dayanand Saraswati founded the Arya Samaj at Bombay in 1875, with the slogan 'Back to the Vedas'. He wrote Satyarth Prakash.","e":"'Back to the Vedas' — Dayanand Saraswati, Arya Samaj, 1875.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0055","q":"The Revolt of 1857 began with the uprising of sepoys at which place?","o":["Barrackpore","Kanpur","Jhansi","Meerut"],"a":3,"x":"The general revolt broke out at Meerut on 10 May 1857, after which the sepoys marched to Delhi. Mangal Pandey's earlier solo act was at Barrackpore in March 1857.","e":"Barrackpore was the spark (Mangal Pandey); Meerut was the fire (10 May 1857).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0056","q":"Who was proclaimed the symbolic leader of the Revolt of 1857 at Delhi?","o":["Bahadur Shah Zafar","Nana Saheb","Tatya Tope","Kunwar Singh"],"a":0,"x":"The rebel sepoys proclaimed the last Mughal emperor, Bahadur Shah Zafar, as their leader at Delhi. He was later exiled to Rangoon, where he died in 1862.","e":"Centres of 1857: Delhi (Bahadur Shah Zafar), Kanpur (Nana Saheb), Jhansi (Rani Lakshmibai), Lucknow (Begum Hazrat Mahal).","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"freedom","n":"Freedom Struggle (1885–1947)","qs":[{"id":"p0057","q":"The Indian National Congress was founded in 1885 by whom?","o":["W. C. Bonnerjee","Dadabhai Naoroji","Surendranath Banerjee","A. O. Hume"],"a":3,"x":"A. O. Hume, a retired British civil servant, founded the Indian National Congress in 1885. Its first session was held in Bombay, with W. C. Bonnerjee as president.","e":"Hume founded it; Bonnerjee presided over the first session at Bombay.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0058","q":"The Partition of Bengal in 1905 was carried out by which Viceroy?","o":["Lord Curzon","Lord Minto","Lord Hardinge","Lord Chelmsford"],"a":0,"x":"Lord Curzon partitioned Bengal in 1905, which set off the Swadeshi movement. The partition was annulled in 1911.","e":"Curzon split Bengal in 1905; the protest that followed was the Swadeshi movement.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0059","q":"At which session did the Indian National Congress adopt 'Purna Swaraj' as its goal?","o":["Lahore, 1929","Nagpur, 1920","Calcutta, 1928","Karachi, 1931"],"a":0,"x":"At the Lahore session of December 1929, presided over by Jawaharlal Nehru, the Congress adopted complete independence as its goal. Independence Day was first observed on 26 January 1930.","e":"Lahore 1929 → Purna Swaraj → 26 January, the date later chosen for the Constitution.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0060","q":"Which committee was appointed by the British government to inquire into the Jallianwala Bagh massacre?","o":["The Simon Commission","The Sargent Committee","The Rowlatt Committee","The Hunter Committee"],"a":3,"x":"The Hunter Committee, headed by Lord William Hunter, was appointed in 1919 to inquire into the firing at Jallianwala Bagh. The Congress set up its own committee because it found the official inquiry inadequate.","e":"The Rowlatt Act led to the protest, Dyer ordered the firing, the Hunter Committee inquired into it.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0061","q":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was called off by Gandhi after which incident?","o":["Jallianwala Bagh massacre","Kakori conspiracy","Simon Commission protest","Chauri Chaura incident"],"a":3,"x":"After a crowd set fire to a police station at Chauri Chaura in February 1922, killing policemen, Gandhi called off the Non-Cooperation Movement because it had turned violent.","e":"Chauri Chaura, February 1922 — the movement stopped because it stopped being non-violent.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0062","q":"The Dandi March, which began the Civil Disobedience Movement, started on which date?","o":["6 April 1930","26 January 1930","12 March 1930","8 August 1942"],"a":2,"x":"Gandhi began the Dandi March from Sabarmati Ashram on 12 March 1930 and broke the salt law at Dandi on 6 April 1930, after walking about 240 miles.","e":"Started 12 March, reached Dandi and made salt on 6 April 1930.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0063","q":"The slogan 'Do or Die' is associated with which movement?","o":["Non-Cooperation Movement","Quit India Movement","Swadeshi Movement","Khilafat Movement"],"a":1,"x":"Gandhi gave the call 'Karo ya Maro' (Do or Die) in his speech at Gowalia Tank, Bombay, on 8 August 1942, launching the Quit India Movement.","e":"Quit India: 8 August 1942, Gowalia Tank, 'Do or Die'.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0064","q":"Who gave the slogan 'Give me blood and I will give you freedom'?","o":["Bhagat Singh","Bal Gangadhar Tilak","Subhas Chandra Bose","Lala Lajpat Rai"],"a":2,"x":"Subhas Chandra Bose gave this call to the Indian National Army in Burma in 1944. He also coined 'Jai Hind' and 'Dilli Chalo'.","e":"Bose: 'Give me blood', 'Jai Hind', 'Dilli Chalo', Azad Hind Fauj.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0065","q":"Who said 'Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it'?","o":["Gopal Krishna Gokhale","Bal Gangadhar Tilak","Bipin Chandra Pal","Aurobindo Ghosh"],"a":1,"x":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak gave this slogan. He was part of the Lal-Bal-Pal trio and started the Ganapati and Shivaji festivals to build public feeling.","e":"Lal (Lala Lajpat Rai), Bal (Tilak), Pal (Bipin Chandra Pal) — the extremist trio.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0066","q":"The Cripps Mission came to India in which year?","o":["1946","1939","1935","1942"],"a":3,"x":"Sir Stafford Cripps came in March 1942 offering dominion status after the war. The Congress rejected it; Gandhi called it 'a post-dated cheque'.","e":"Cripps 1942 (rejected) → Quit India 1942; Cabinet Mission came in 1946.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0067","q":"Who was the last Viceroy of India?","o":["Lord Wavell","Lord Linlithgow","C. Rajagopalachari","Lord Mountbatten"],"a":3,"x":"Lord Mountbatten was the last Viceroy and the first Governor-General of independent India. C. Rajagopalachari was the last Governor-General, and India has never had a Viceroy since 1947.","e":"Mountbatten: last Viceroy; Rajagopalachari: last Governor-General.","src":["Practice question"]}]}]},{"id":"pol","n":"Polity","tab":"Polity","w":"The Constitution, rights, Parliament, courts, states and constitutional bodies.","topics":[{"id":"constitution","n":"Making and Features of the Constitution","qs":[{"id":"p0068","q":"The Constituent Assembly of India met for the first time on which date?","o":["9 December 1946","26 November 1949","26 January 1950","15 August 1947"],"a":0,"x":"The Constituent Assembly first met on 9 December 1946, with Sachchidananda Sinha as temporary chairman. Rajendra Prasad was elected its permanent president on 11 December 1946.","e":"First sitting 9 Dec 1946, Constitution adopted 26 Nov 1949, in force 26 Jan 1950.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0069","q":"Who was the chairman of the Drafting Committee of the Indian Constitution?","o":["B. R. Ambedkar","Rajendra Prasad","Jawaharlal Nehru","K. M. Munshi"],"a":0,"x":"B. R. Ambedkar chaired the seven-member Drafting Committee set up on 29 August 1947. Rajendra Prasad was president of the Constituent Assembly itself.","e":"Ambedkar chaired the drafting; Rajendra Prasad chaired the Assembly.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0070","q":"The words 'Socialist', 'Secular' and 'Integrity' were added to the Preamble by which amendment?","o":["44th Amendment","24th Amendment","42nd Amendment","52nd Amendment"],"a":2,"x":"The 42nd Amendment of 1976 inserted 'Socialist' and 'Secular' into the description of the Republic and added 'and integrity' after 'unity'.","e":"The 42nd is called the 'Mini-Constitution' — it also added the Fundamental Duties.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0071","q":"The idea of Directive Principles of State Policy in the Indian Constitution was borrowed from which country?","o":["United States","Canada","Australia","Ireland"],"a":3,"x":"The Directive Principles in Part IV were taken from the Irish Constitution, which had in turn drawn on the Spanish one.","e":"Ireland: DPSP. USA: Fundamental Rights. UK: parliamentary system. Canada: strong centre.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0072","q":"How many schedules does the Constitution of India have at present?","o":["Twelve","Ten","Eight","Fourteen"],"a":0,"x":"The Constitution began with eight schedules; it now has twelve. The ninth, tenth, eleventh and twelfth were added by later amendments.","e":"8th = languages, 9th = land reform laws, 10th = anti-defection, 11th = panchayats, 12th = municipalities.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"rights","n":"Citizenship, Rights, DPSP and Duties","qs":[{"id":"p0073","q":"Which Article of the Constitution abolishes untouchability?","o":["Article 15","Article 16","Article 17","Article 18"],"a":2,"x":"Article 17 abolishes untouchability and forbids its practice in any form; enforcing any disability arising out of it is an offence punishable by law.","e":"14 equality, 15 no discrimination, 16 equal opportunity, 17 untouchability, 18 titles.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0074","q":"Dr B. R. Ambedkar described which Article as the 'heart and soul' of the Constitution?","o":["Article 32","Article 21","Article 14","Article 19"],"a":0,"x":"Article 32 gives the right to move the Supreme Court directly for the enforcement of Fundamental Rights. Ambedkar called it the heart and soul of the Constitution because the other rights would be empty without it.","e":"Article 32 is itself a Fundamental Right — the right to a remedy.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0075","q":"The Right to Education was made a Fundamental Right by which amendment?","o":["73rd Amendment","86th Amendment","44th Amendment","61st Amendment"],"a":1,"x":"The 86th Amendment of 2002 inserted Article 21A, making free and compulsory education a Fundamental Right for children aged six to fourteen.","e":"86th Amendment: Article 21A, ages 6 to 14, and the duty of parents in Article 51A(k).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0076","q":"The Right to Property ceased to be a Fundamental Right after which amendment?","o":["42nd Amendment","24th Amendment","25th Amendment","44th Amendment"],"a":3,"x":"The 44th Amendment of 1978 removed the Right to Property from the list of Fundamental Rights. It is now a constitutional legal right under Article 300A.","e":"Property moved out of Fundamental Rights in 1978 and became Article 300A.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0077","q":"Fundamental Duties were added to the Constitution on the recommendation of which committee?","o":["Sarkaria Commission","Balwant Rai Mehta Committee","Ashok Mehta Committee","Swaran Singh Committee"],"a":3,"x":"The Swaran Singh Committee recommended Fundamental Duties, which the 42nd Amendment of 1976 added as Part IVA, Article 51A.","e":"Swaran Singh → 42nd Amendment → Part IVA → Article 51A (now eleven duties).","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"union","n":"President, Prime Minister and Parliament","qs":[{"id":"p0078","q":"The President of India is elected by an electoral college that includes which of the following?","o":["All members of Parliament and all MLAs","Elected members of both Houses of Parliament and elected members of state legislative assemblies","Only the members of the Lok Sabha","Members of the Lok Sabha and state legislative councils"],"a":1,"x":"Under Article 54 the electoral college has the elected members of both Houses of Parliament and the elected members of the legislative assemblies of the states, including Delhi and Puducherry. Nominated members and legislative council members do not vote.","e":"Nominated members cannot vote for the President — only elected ones.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0079","q":"What is the minimum age to be eligible for election as President of India?","o":["25 years","30 years","35 years","40 years"],"a":2,"x":"Article 58 requires the President to be a citizen of India, at least 35 years old, and qualified for election to the Lok Sabha.","e":"35 for President and Vice-President, 30 for Rajya Sabha, 25 for Lok Sabha.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0080","q":"The Council of Ministers of the Union is collectively responsible to which body?","o":["The President","The House of the People (Lok Sabha)","The Rajya Sabha","Both Houses of Parliament"],"a":1,"x":"Article 75(3) makes the Council of Ministers collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha alone. If the Lok Sabha passes a no-confidence motion, the whole ministry must resign.","e":"Only the Lok Sabha can bring the government down — the Rajya Sabha cannot.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0081","q":"What is the maximum strength of the Rajya Sabha as fixed by the Constitution?","o":["250","245","238","552"],"a":0,"x":"Article 80 fixes the maximum strength of the Rajya Sabha at 250 — up to 238 representing the states and union territories, plus 12 nominated by the President.","e":"Rajya Sabha ceiling 250 (238 + 12 nominated); Lok Sabha ceiling 550.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0082","q":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in which House?","o":["Rajya Sabha","Lok Sabha","Either House","A joint sitting"],"a":1,"x":"Under Article 110 a Money Bill may be introduced only in the Lok Sabha, and only on the President's recommendation. The Rajya Sabha can keep it for at most fourteen days and may only suggest changes.","e":"Money Bills: Lok Sabha only, Rajya Sabha gets 14 days and no veto.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"judiciary","n":"Judiciary","qs":[{"id":"p0083","q":"At what age does a judge of the Supreme Court of India retire?","o":["62 years","60 years","65 years","70 years"],"a":2,"x":"A Supreme Court judge holds office until the age of 65 under Article 124. A High Court judge retires at 62 under Article 217.","e":"Supreme Court 65, High Court 62.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0084","q":"Which writ is issued to free a person who has been unlawfully detained?","o":["Mandamus","Quo Warranto","Certiorari","Habeas Corpus"],"a":3,"x":"Habeas Corpus means 'you may have the body'. The court orders the detaining authority to produce the person and justify the detention.","e":"Habeas Corpus = produce the body; Mandamus = do your duty; Quo Warranto = by what authority.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0085","q":"Which is the oldest High Court in India?","o":["Calcutta High Court","Bombay High Court","Madras High Court","Allahabad High Court"],"a":0,"x":"The Calcutta High Court was established in 1862 and is the oldest in India. Bombay and Madras were set up in the same year but Calcutta was constituted first.","e":"Calcutta, Bombay and Madras all date from 1862; Calcutta came first.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"states","n":"States, Union Territories, Delhi and Local Bodies","qs":[{"id":"p0086","q":"Panchayati Raj was given constitutional status by which amendment?","o":["74th Amendment","72nd Amendment","65th Amendment","73rd Amendment"],"a":3,"x":"The 73rd Amendment of 1992 added Part IX and the Eleventh Schedule, giving panchayats constitutional status. The 74th did the same for urban local bodies.","e":"73rd = villages (11th Schedule, 29 subjects); 74th = towns (12th Schedule, 18 subjects).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0087","q":"Which Article gives the National Capital Territory of Delhi its special constitutional position?","o":["Article 370","Article 371","Article 239AA","Article 243"],"a":2,"x":"Article 239AA, inserted by the 69th Amendment of 1991, created the National Capital Territory of Delhi with a legislative assembly and a council of ministers.","e":"69th Amendment, 1991: Delhi became the NCT under Article 239AA.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"federal","n":"Centre–State Relations and Emergency","qs":[{"id":"p0088","q":"President's Rule in a state is imposed under which Article?","o":["Article 352","Article 360","Article 356","Article 365"],"a":2,"x":"Article 356 allows the President to take over a state's government when its constitutional machinery fails. Article 352 covers national emergency and Article 360 financial emergency.","e":"352 national, 356 state, 360 financial.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0089","q":"The Concurrent List in the Seventh Schedule was borrowed from the constitution of which country?","o":["Canada","Australia","United States","Ireland"],"a":1,"x":"The idea of a Concurrent List, on which both the Union and the states may legislate, was taken from the Australian Constitution.","e":"Australia gave India the Concurrent List and freedom of trade between states.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"bodies","n":"Constitutional Bodies, Acts and Elections","qs":[{"id":"p0090","q":"The Comptroller and Auditor General of India is appointed under which Article?","o":["Article 324","Article 280","Article 148","Article 315"],"a":2,"x":"Article 148 provides for the Comptroller and Auditor General, appointed by the President and holding office for six years or until the age of 65, whichever is earlier.","e":"148 CAG, 280 Finance Commission, 315 UPSC, 324 Election Commission.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0091","q":"The Election Commission of India is established under which Article of the Constitution?","o":["Article 320","Article 324","Article 280","Article 148"],"a":1,"x":"Article 324 vests the superintendence, direction and control of elections to Parliament, state legislatures and the offices of President and Vice-President in the Election Commission.","e":"Article 324 — one Commission for Parliament, states, President and Vice-President.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0092","q":"The anti-defection law is contained in which Schedule of the Constitution?","o":["Ninth Schedule","Eleventh Schedule","Tenth Schedule","Eighth Schedule"],"a":2,"x":"The Tenth Schedule, added by the 52nd Amendment of 1985, sets out the grounds on which a member of a legislature is disqualified for defection.","e":"52nd Amendment 1985 → Tenth Schedule → anti-defection.","src":["Practice question"]}]}]},{"id":"geo","n":"Geography","tab":"Geography","w":"Earth, physical geography, India, resources, population and the world.","topics":[{"id":"earth","n":"Earth and the Solar System","qs":[{"id":"p0093","q":"Which planet is known as the Morning Star as well as the Evening Star?","o":["Venus","Mercury","Mars","Jupiter"],"a":0,"x":"Venus is the brightest planet in our sky and is seen just before sunrise or just after sunset, which gives it both names. It is also the hottest planet.","e":"Venus: brightest, hottest, and spins the 'wrong' way, east to west.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0094","q":"Light from the Sun takes approximately how long to reach the Earth?","o":["1.3 seconds","8 minutes 20 seconds","2 hours","42 minutes"],"a":1,"x":"Sunlight covers the roughly 150 million km to Earth in about 8 minutes and 20 seconds. Light from the Moon takes about 1.3 seconds.","e":"Sun about 8 minutes, Moon about 1.3 seconds.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0095","q":"The Tropic of Cancer passes through how many Indian states?","o":["Six","Eight","Seven","Nine"],"a":1,"x":"The Tropic of Cancer crosses eight states: Gujarat, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura and Mizoram.","e":"Remember west to east: Gujarat, Rajasthan, MP, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura, Mizoram.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"physical","n":"Physical Geography","qs":[{"id":"p0096","q":"The ozone layer is found in which layer of the atmosphere?","o":["Troposphere","Mesosphere","Thermosphere","Stratosphere"],"a":3,"x":"The ozone layer lies in the stratosphere, roughly 15 to 35 km above the surface, where it absorbs most ultraviolet radiation from the Sun.","e":"Weather happens in the troposphere; ozone and jet aircraft belong to the stratosphere.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0097","q":"Most of the world's volcanoes and earthquakes occur around which feature?","o":["The Pacific Ring of Fire","The Mid-Atlantic Ridge","The Great Rift Valley","The Mariana Trench"],"a":0,"x":"The Pacific Ring of Fire is the belt of plate boundaries around the Pacific Ocean, home to about three-quarters of the world's active volcanoes and most large earthquakes.","e":"Ring of Fire = the rim of the Pacific plate, from Chile up to Alaska and down to Indonesia.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"india","n":"India: Relief, Rivers and Climate","qs":[{"id":"p0098","q":"The standard meridian of India passes through which place?","o":["Ujjain","Nagpur","Mirzapur","Kanpur"],"a":2,"x":"India's standard meridian is 82 degrees 30 minutes East, which passes through Mirzapur in Uttar Pradesh. Indian Standard Time is five and a half hours ahead of GMT.","e":"82 degrees 30 minutes E through Mirzapur gives IST = GMT + 5:30.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0099","q":"Which is the highest mountain peak lying entirely within India?","o":["K2","Kanchenjunga","Anamudi","Nanda Devi"],"a":3,"x":"Nanda Devi in Uttarakhand, at 7,816 m, is the highest peak lying wholly inside India. Kanchenjunga is higher but sits on the India-Nepal border, and K2 is in territory India claims but does not administer.","e":"Nanda Devi — highest peak completely inside India's own territory.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0100","q":"Chilika Lake, the largest brackish water lagoon in India, lies in which state?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Tamil Nadu","West Bengal","Odisha"],"a":3,"x":"Chilika Lake on the Odisha coast is India's largest brackish water lagoon and a Ramsar site known for migratory birds and Irrawaddy dolphins.","e":"Chilika brackish (Odisha), Wular freshwater (Jammu and Kashmir), Sambhar salt (Rajasthan).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0101","q":"Loktak Lake, famous for its floating islands called phumdis, is located in which state?","o":["Assam","Meghalaya","Tripura","Manipur"],"a":3,"x":"Loktak Lake in Manipur is the largest freshwater lake in north-east India. Its floating mats of vegetation, called phumdis, carry Keibul Lamjao, the world's only floating national park.","e":"Loktak, Manipur — phumdis and the floating national park where the sangai deer lives.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0102","q":"The southernmost point of India's territory, Indira Point, is located in which island group?","o":["Lakshadweep","Nicobar Islands","Andaman Islands","Daman and Diu"],"a":1,"x":"Indira Point is at the southern tip of Great Nicobar Island in the Nicobar group. Kanyakumari is the southernmost point of the Indian mainland.","e":"Indira Point = southernmost of India; Kanyakumari = southernmost of the mainland.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"resources","n":"Resources, Agriculture and Industry","qs":[{"id":"p0103","q":"The Khetri region of Rajasthan is known for the mining of which mineral?","o":["Copper","Iron ore","Bauxite","Manganese"],"a":0,"x":"The Khetri belt in Jhunjhunu district, Rajasthan, is one of India's main copper-producing areas, worked by Hindustan Copper Limited.","e":"Khetri copper (Rajasthan), Kolar gold (Karnataka), Jharia coal (Jharkhand).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0104","q":"The White Revolution in India is associated with the production of which commodity?","o":["Eggs","Cotton","Milk","Oilseeds"],"a":2,"x":"The White Revolution, run as Operation Flood from 1970 under Verghese Kurien, made India the world's largest milk producer and built the Amul dairy cooperative model.","e":"White milk, Green foodgrains, Blue fish, Yellow oilseeds, Pink prawns.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0105","q":"Black soil, also called regur soil, is best suited for the cultivation of which crop?","o":["Rice","Cotton","Tea","Jute"],"a":1,"x":"Black or regur soil, formed from the lava of the Deccan Trap, holds moisture well and is the classic cotton soil of Maharashtra, Gujarat and Madhya Pradesh.","e":"Regur is also simply called 'black cotton soil'.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"people","n":"Population and Census","qs":[{"id":"p0106","q":"According to Census 2011, which state has the highest literacy rate in India?","o":["Kerala","Mizoram","Goa","Tripura"],"a":0,"x":"Kerala recorded a literacy rate of about 94 per cent in Census 2011, the highest among the states; Bihar was the lowest.","e":"Census 2011: Kerala highest literacy, Bihar lowest, national figure about 74 per cent.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0107","q":"According to Census 2011, which state has the highest population density in India?","o":["Uttar Pradesh","West Bengal","Kerala","Bihar"],"a":3,"x":"Bihar had the highest population density among states in Census 2011, at about 1,106 persons per square kilometre. Delhi has a higher density but is a union territory.","e":"Most people: Uttar Pradesh. Most crowded state: Bihar.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"world","n":"World and Neighbours","qs":[{"id":"p0108","q":"Which is the longest river in the world?","o":["Amazon","Nile","Yangtze","Mississippi"],"a":1,"x":"The Nile in Africa, about 6,650 km long, is conventionally taken as the world's longest river. The Amazon carries the greatest volume of water.","e":"Nile longest, Amazon largest by volume.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0109","q":"India shares its longest international land border with which country?","o":["China","Pakistan","Nepal","Bangladesh"],"a":3,"x":"India's border with Bangladesh runs about 4,096 km, the longest it shares with any neighbour. The shortest is with Afghanistan.","e":"Longest: Bangladesh. Shortest: Afghanistan.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0110","q":"The McMahon Line forms the boundary between India and which country?","o":["Pakistan","Nepal","China","Myanmar"],"a":2,"x":"The McMahon Line, drawn at the Simla Convention of 1914, is the boundary between India and China in the eastern sector, along Arunachal Pradesh.","e":"Radcliffe: India-Pakistan. McMahon: India-China. Durand: Pakistan-Afghanistan.","src":["Practice question"]}]}]},{"id":"eco","n":"Economy","tab":"Economy","w":"Static economy only: concepts, banking, planning and long-running schemes.","topics":[{"id":"basics","n":"Concepts and National Income","qs":[{"id":"p0111","q":"Net National Product (NNP) is obtained by subtracting what from Gross National Product?","o":["Indirect taxes","Subsidies","Depreciation","Net factor income from abroad"],"a":2,"x":"NNP = GNP minus depreciation, the wear and tear of capital goods during the year. Subtracting net indirect taxes from NNP at market prices gives national income, or NNP at factor cost.","e":"Gross becomes Net when you take out depreciation.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0112","q":"A situation of rising prices together with stagnant output and high unemployment is called what?","o":["Deflation","Disinflation","Reflation","Stagflation"],"a":3,"x":"Stagflation combines stagnation with inflation — prices keep rising even though growth has stalled and unemployment is high.","e":"Stagnation + inflation = stagflation.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0113","q":"The Lorenz curve is used to show what?","o":["Income inequality","Inflation over time","Demand and supply","The business cycle"],"a":0,"x":"The Lorenz curve plots the share of income against the share of population. The further it bends away from the line of equality, the greater the inequality; the Gini coefficient measures that gap.","e":"Lorenz curve draws inequality; the Gini coefficient puts a number on it.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0114","q":"Engel's law relates a rise in income to a fall in which share of spending?","o":["Spending on housing","Spending on travel","Spending on food","Spending on education"],"a":2,"x":"Engel's law states that as a family's income rises, the proportion of income spent on food falls, even though the amount spent may rise.","e":"Richer households still buy food — it just takes up a smaller slice of the budget.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0115","q":"Goods whose demand falls as consumer income rises are called what?","o":["Giffen goods","Veblen goods","Complementary goods","Inferior goods"],"a":3,"x":"An inferior good is one people buy less of as they grow richer, because they switch to a better substitute — coarse grain giving way to finer grain, for example.","e":"Inferior good: more income, less demand.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"money","n":"Money, Banking and the RBI","qs":[{"id":"p0116","q":"The Reserve Bank of India was established in which year?","o":["1935","1949","1947","1955"],"a":0,"x":"The RBI was set up on 1 April 1935 under the Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934, and was nationalised on 1 January 1949.","e":"Act 1934, started 1935, nationalised 1949.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0117","q":"The repo rate is the rate at which what happens?","o":["Commercial banks lend to their best customers","Banks lend to one another overnight","The RBI lends short-term funds to commercial banks","The RBI borrows from commercial banks"],"a":2,"x":"The repo rate is the rate at which the RBI lends short-term money to commercial banks against government securities. The rate at which the RBI borrows from banks is the reverse repo rate.","e":"Repo: RBI lends. Reverse repo: RBI borrows.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0118","q":"The portion of a bank's deposits that it must keep as cash with the RBI is called what?","o":["Statutory Liquidity Ratio","Bank Rate","Marginal Standing Facility","Cash Reserve Ratio"],"a":3,"x":"The Cash Reserve Ratio is the share of net demand and time liabilities a bank must hold in cash with the RBI. The Statutory Liquidity Ratio is the share it must keep in its own safe assets such as government securities and gold.","e":"CRR sits with the RBI as cash; SLR stays with the bank as safe assets.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0119","q":"How many commercial banks were nationalised in India in the first round of 1969?","o":["Fourteen","Six","Twelve","Twenty"],"a":0,"x":"Fourteen major commercial banks were nationalised in July 1969; six more followed in April 1980.","e":"14 in 1969, 6 in 1980 — twenty banks in all.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0120","q":"Which institution is the apex bank for agriculture and rural development in India?","o":["SIDBI","EXIM Bank","SEBI","NABARD"],"a":3,"x":"NABARD, the National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development, was set up in 1982 as the apex body for rural credit and development finance.","e":"NABARD rural, SIDBI small industry, EXIM foreign trade.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0121","q":"Gresham's law in economics states that what happens?","o":["Bad money drives out good money","Supply creates its own demand","Demand falls as price rises","Savings always equal investment"],"a":0,"x":"Gresham's law says that when two kinds of money of the same legal value circulate together, people spend the poorer one and hoard the better one, so bad money drives good money out of circulation.","e":"People keep the good coin and pass on the worn one.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"planning","n":"Planning, Budget and Reforms","qs":[{"id":"p0122","q":"The First Five Year Plan of India gave the highest priority to which sector?","o":["Heavy industry","Agriculture and irrigation","Services","Export promotion"],"a":1,"x":"The First Five Year Plan (1951-56) was based on the Harrod-Domar model and concentrated on agriculture, irrigation and power after the disruption of Partition. It met its targets.","e":"First Plan farms (Harrod-Domar); Second Plan factories (Mahalanobis).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0123","q":"The Second Five Year Plan of India was based on the model developed by whom?","o":["Harrod and Domar","P. C. Mahalanobis","Amartya Sen","M. Visvesvaraya"],"a":1,"x":"The Second Plan (1956-61) followed P. C. Mahalanobis's model, which put heavy and basic industry at the centre of growth. Steel plants at Bhilai, Rourkela and Durgapur came from it.","e":"Mahalanobis = heavy industry = the Second Plan.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0124","q":"NITI Aayog replaced which body on 1 January 2015?","o":["Planning Commission","Finance Commission","National Development Council","Economic Advisory Council"],"a":0,"x":"NITI Aayog — the National Institution for Transforming India — replaced the Planning Commission, which had been set up in 1950. The Prime Minister chairs it.","e":"Planning Commission 1950 to 2014; NITI Aayog from 1 January 2015.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0125","q":"Under which Article does the President cause the annual financial statement, or Union Budget, to be laid before Parliament?","o":["Article 110","Article 266","Article 112","Article 280"],"a":2,"x":"Article 112 requires the annual financial statement of estimated receipts and expenditure to be laid before both Houses for every financial year. The Constitution never uses the word 'Budget'.","e":"112 Budget, 110 Money Bill, 266 Consolidated Fund, 280 Finance Commission.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0126","q":"The Goods and Services Tax came into force in India on which date?","o":["1 April 2017","1 July 2017","1 January 2018","1 April 2016"],"a":1,"x":"GST came into force on 1 July 2017 under the 101st Constitutional Amendment Act of 2016, replacing a long list of central and state indirect taxes.","e":"101st Amendment (2016) → GST from 1 July 2017.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"schemes","n":"Government Schemes and Policies","qs":[{"id":"p0127","q":"MGNREGA guarantees how many days of wage employment in a financial year to a rural household?","o":["100 days","50 days","150 days","200 days"],"a":0,"x":"The Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act, 2005, guarantees at least 100 days of unskilled manual work in a financial year to every rural household whose adults volunteer for it.","e":"100 days, rural household, unskilled manual work, on demand.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0128","q":"The Green Revolution in India is particularly associated with which crop?","o":["Jute","Wheat","Sugarcane","Tea"],"a":1,"x":"The Green Revolution of the late 1960s rested on high-yielding dwarf varieties of wheat, chiefly in Punjab, Haryana and western Uttar Pradesh. M. S. Swaminathan led the Indian effort and Norman Borlaug bred the varieties.","e":"Swaminathan in India, Borlaug abroad, wheat in Punjab.","src":["Practice question"]}]}]},{"id":"phy","n":"Physics","tab":"Physics","w":"Motion, heat and sound, light, electricity and magnetism.","topics":[{"id":"mechanics","n":"Motion, Force, Units and Measurement","qs":[{"id":"p0129","q":"How many base units are there in the International System of Units (SI)?","o":["Five","Six","Seven","Nine"],"a":2,"x":"The SI has seven base units: metre, kilogram, second, ampere, kelvin, mole and candela. All other units are derived from these.","e":"Seven base units — length, mass, time, current, temperature, amount, light.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0130","q":"Newton's first law of motion is also known as the law of what?","o":["Acceleration","Action and reaction","Conservation of momentum","Inertia"],"a":3,"x":"Newton's first law says a body stays at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless an external force acts on it. That tendency to resist a change of state is inertia.","e":"First law inertia, second law F = ma, third law action and reaction.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0131","q":"What is the SI unit of pressure?","o":["Newton","Pascal","Joule","Watt"],"a":1,"x":"Pressure is force per unit area, so its SI unit is the pascal, equal to one newton per square metre.","e":"Force newton, pressure pascal, work joule, power watt.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0132","q":"The working of a hydraulic brake is based on which principle?","o":["Archimedes' principle","Bernoulli's principle","Pascal's law","Newton's third law"],"a":2,"x":"Pascal's law says pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished in every direction. Hydraulic brakes and hydraulic lifts both use it.","e":"Pascal's law: a small push on a small piston becomes a big push on a big one.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"heat","n":"Heat, Sound and Thermodynamics","qs":[{"id":"p0133","q":"Sound cannot travel through which of the following?","o":["Solids","Liquids","Vacuum","Gases"],"a":2,"x":"Sound is a mechanical wave and needs a material medium to carry it. A vacuum has no particles to vibrate, so sound cannot pass through it. Sound travels fastest in solids.","e":"No medium, no sound — which is why space is silent.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0134","q":"Sound waves of frequency above 20,000 hertz are called what?","o":["Infrasonic","Ultrasonic","Supersonic","Audible"],"a":1,"x":"The human ear hears roughly 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz. Above that range the waves are ultrasonic; below it they are infrasonic. 'Supersonic' describes speed, not frequency.","e":"Ultrasound is used in medical scans and in cleaning; bats use it to navigate.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0135","q":"Heat transfer by the actual movement of heated particles of a fluid is called what?","o":["Conduction","Convection","Radiation","Insulation"],"a":1,"x":"In convection, heated fluid becomes less dense, rises and carries heat with it, while cooler fluid sinks to take its place. Land and sea breezes work this way.","e":"Conduction through solids, convection in fluids, radiation needs no medium.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"light","n":"Light and Optics","qs":[{"id":"p0136","q":"The blue colour of the clear sky is caused by which phenomenon?","o":["Reflection of light","Refraction of light","Scattering of light","Total internal reflection"],"a":2,"x":"Air molecules scatter shorter wavelengths much more strongly than longer ones, so blue light is spread across the sky. The same effect makes the setting Sun look red.","e":"Blue scatters most, red scatters least — that is why sunsets are red.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0137","q":"A mirage seen on a hot road is caused by which phenomenon?","o":["Diffraction","Polarisation","Total internal reflection","Interference"],"a":2,"x":"Hot air near the road is less dense than the air above it. Light from the sky bends gradually and is then totally internally reflected, so the road appears to carry a pool of water.","e":"Mirage = refraction through layers of air ending in total internal reflection.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0138","q":"Short-sightedness, or myopia, is corrected using which type of lens?","o":["Convex lens","Cylindrical lens","Bifocal lens","Concave lens"],"a":3,"x":"In myopia the image forms in front of the retina, so a diverging — concave — lens is used to push it back. Long-sightedness is corrected with a convex lens.","e":"Myopia concave, hypermetropia convex, astigmatism cylindrical.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"electricity","n":"Electricity and Magnetism","qs":[{"id":"p0139","q":"According to Ohm's law, the current through a conductor is directly proportional to what, at constant temperature?","o":["The potential difference across it","Its resistance","Its length","Its cross-sectional area"],"a":0,"x":"Ohm's law states V = IR: at constant temperature the current through a conductor is proportional to the potential difference across its ends.","e":"V = IR — voltage up, current up; resistance up, current down.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0140","q":"Domestic electrical appliances are connected in which arrangement?","o":["Series","Mixed","Star","Parallel"],"a":3,"x":"House wiring uses parallel connections so each appliance gets the full supply voltage and can be switched on or off without affecting the others.","e":"Parallel at home: one bulb failing does not stop the rest.","src":["Practice question"]}]}]},{"id":"chem","n":"Chemistry","tab":"Chemistry","w":"Matter and solutions, reactions, and chemistry in daily life.","topics":[{"id":"matter","n":"Matter, Atoms and Solutions","qs":[{"id":"p0141","q":"The atomic number of an element is equal to the number of what?","o":["Neutrons in its nucleus","Protons in its nucleus","Protons and neutrons together","Electrons in the outermost shell"],"a":1,"x":"The atomic number is the number of protons in the nucleus, and it fixes the identity of the element. Protons plus neutrons give the mass number.","e":"Atomic number = protons; mass number = protons + neutrons.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0142","q":"Who discovered the nucleus of the atom through the gold foil experiment?","o":["J. J. Thomson","Niels Bohr","Ernest Rutherford","John Dalton"],"a":2,"x":"Rutherford's alpha-particle scattering experiment on thin gold foil in 1911 showed that an atom's positive charge and nearly all its mass sit in a tiny central nucleus.","e":"Thomson found the electron, Rutherford the nucleus, Chadwick the neutron.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0143","q":"The modern periodic law states that the properties of elements are a periodic function of what?","o":["Atomic mass","Valency","Atomic number","Number of neutrons"],"a":2,"x":"Moseley's work showed that properties repeat according to atomic number, not atomic mass as Mendeleev had assumed. That change resolved the anomalies in Mendeleev's table.","e":"Mendeleev used atomic mass; Moseley corrected it to atomic number.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0144","q":"Which is the chemical symbol for potassium?","o":["P","K","Pt","Po"],"a":1,"x":"Potassium's symbol K comes from its Latin name kalium. P is phosphorus, Pt is platinum and Po is polonium.","e":"Latin-based symbols: K kalium, Na natrium, Fe ferrum, Au aurum, Ag argentum, Pb plumbum.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0145","q":"Diamond and graphite are both forms of which element?","o":["Silicon","Sulphur","Carbon","Boron"],"a":2,"x":"Diamond and graphite are allotropes of carbon. In diamond each carbon is bonded to four others, which makes it the hardest natural substance; graphite's layered structure makes it soft and able to conduct electricity.","e":"Same element, different arrangement — hardest substance and pencil lead both pure carbon.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"reactions","n":"Reactions, Acids and Bases","qs":[{"id":"p0146","q":"What is the pH value of a neutral solution at 25 degrees Celsius?","o":["0","7","14","1"],"a":1,"x":"The pH scale runs from 0 to 14; 7 is neutral, below 7 is acidic and above 7 is basic. Pure water at 25 degrees Celsius has a pH of 7.","e":"Below 7 sour, 7 neutral, above 7 bitter and slippery.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0147","q":"The rusting of iron is an example of which type of reaction?","o":["Reduction","Neutralisation","Oxidation","Sublimation"],"a":2,"x":"Rust is hydrated iron oxide, formed when iron is oxidised in the presence of both oxygen and moisture. Coating the iron with zinc — galvanisation — prevents it.","e":"Rusting needs air and water together; remove either and iron does not rust.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0148","q":"The chemical name of baking soda is what?","o":["Sodium carbonate","Sodium bicarbonate","Calcium carbonate","Sodium hydroxide"],"a":1,"x":"Baking soda is sodium bicarbonate, NaHCO3. Washing soda is sodium carbonate decahydrate, Na2CO3.10H2O.","e":"Baking soda bicarbonate; washing soda carbonate.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"everyday","n":"Chemistry in Everyday Life","qs":[{"id":"p0149","q":"The acid present in a lemon is which one?","o":["Citric acid","Acetic acid","Lactic acid","Oxalic acid"],"a":0,"x":"Lemons and other citrus fruits contain citric acid. Vinegar has acetic acid, curd has lactic acid and tomatoes contain oxalic acid.","e":"Lemon citric, vinegar acetic, curd lactic, ant sting formic, tamarind tartaric.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0150","q":"Brass is an alloy of copper and which other metal?","o":["Tin","Zinc","Lead","Nickel"],"a":1,"x":"Brass is copper with zinc; bronze is copper with tin. Steel is iron with carbon, and stainless steel adds chromium and nickel.","e":"Brass has zinc, bronze has tin — both start with copper.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0151","q":"Dry ice is the solid form of which substance?","o":["Water","Ammonia","Nitrogen","Carbon dioxide"],"a":3,"x":"Dry ice is solid carbon dioxide. It sublimes straight from solid to gas without melting, which is why it leaves no liquid behind.","e":"Dry ice is 'dry' because it never turns into a liquid.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0152","q":"The main constituent of Compressed Natural Gas (CNG) is which gas?","o":["Butane","Propane","Ethane","Methane"],"a":3,"x":"CNG is mostly methane. LPG, the cooking gas in cylinders, is mainly a mixture of butane and propane.","e":"CNG methane for vehicles; LPG butane and propane for the kitchen.","src":["Practice question"]}]}]},{"id":"bio","n":"Biology","tab":"Biology","w":"Cells and genetics, the human body, health, plants and the living world.","topics":[{"id":"cell","n":"Cell, Genetics and DNA","qs":[{"id":"p0153","q":"Who discovered the cell in 1665 while examining a thin slice of cork?","o":["Robert Brown","Anton van Leeuwenhoek","Theodor Schwann","Robert Hooke"],"a":3,"x":"Robert Hooke saw box-like compartments in cork under his microscope and named them cells, after the small rooms of a monastery. Leeuwenhoek was the first to see living cells.","e":"Hooke saw dead cork cells; Leeuwenhoek saw living ones.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0154","q":"Which cell organelle is called the powerhouse of the cell?","o":["Mitochondrion","Ribosome","Golgi body","Lysosome"],"a":0,"x":"Mitochondria carry out cellular respiration and release energy as ATP, which is why they are called the powerhouse of the cell. They have their own DNA.","e":"Mitochondria make ATP; ribosomes make protein; lysosomes digest.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0155","q":"The nucleus of the cell was discovered by whom?","o":["Robert Hooke","Rudolf Virchow","Robert Brown","Matthias Schleiden"],"a":2,"x":"Robert Brown described the cell nucleus in 1831 while studying orchid cells. He is also remembered for Brownian motion.","e":"Robert Brown: nucleus and Brownian motion.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0156","q":"The double helix structure of DNA was proposed in 1953 by whom?","o":["Watson and Crick","Mendel and Morgan","Darwin and Wallace","Pasteur and Koch"],"a":0,"x":"James Watson and Francis Crick proposed the double helix in 1953, using X-ray diffraction data from Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins.","e":"Two strands, twisted — Watson and Crick, 1953.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"human","n":"Human Body","qs":[{"id":"p0157","q":"How many bones are there in the body of an adult human being?","o":["212","206","300","196"],"a":1,"x":"An adult human skeleton has 206 bones. A newborn has around 300, many of which fuse together as the child grows.","e":"Baby about 300, adult 206 — bones fuse as you grow.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0158","q":"How many chambers does the human heart have?","o":["Two","Three","Six","Four"],"a":3,"x":"The human heart has four chambers: two upper atria and two lower ventricles. The right side pumps blood to the lungs and the left side pumps it to the rest of the body.","e":"Four chambers: two atria receive, two ventricles pump; the left ventricle has the thickest wall.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0159","q":"Which is the smallest bone in the human body?","o":["Femur","Vomer","Stapes","Lacrimal"],"a":2,"x":"The stapes, or stirrup bone, in the middle ear is the smallest bone in the body. The femur, or thigh bone, is the longest.","e":"Smallest stapes in the ear; longest femur in the thigh.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0160","q":"Insulin is secreted by which organ?","o":["Pancreas","Liver","Kidney","Thyroid"],"a":0,"x":"The beta cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas secrete insulin, which lowers blood glucose. A shortage of it causes diabetes mellitus.","e":"Pancreas: insulin lowers sugar, glucagon raises it.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0161","q":"Human blood groups were discovered by whom?","o":["Karl Landsteiner","Alexander Fleming","William Harvey","Louis Pasteur"],"a":0,"x":"Karl Landsteiner discovered the ABO blood group system in 1901 and received the Nobel Prize for it in 1930. He later helped identify the Rh factor.","e":"Landsteiner ABO; Harvey described blood circulation.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"health","n":"Nutrition and Diseases","qs":[{"id":"p0162","q":"Malaria is transmitted by the bite of which organism?","o":["Female Aedes mosquito","Culex mosquito","Housefly","Female Anopheles mosquito"],"a":3,"x":"The female Anopheles mosquito transmits the Plasmodium parasite that causes malaria. Aedes aegypti spreads dengue and chikungunya.","e":"Anopheles malaria, Aedes dengue, Culex filaria.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0163","q":"The deficiency of which vitamin causes scurvy?","o":["Vitamin A","Vitamin D","Vitamin K","Vitamin C"],"a":3,"x":"Scurvy, with bleeding gums and slow healing, comes from a shortage of vitamin C, or ascorbic acid, found in citrus fruits and amla.","e":"A night blindness, B1 beriberi, C scurvy, D rickets, K poor clotting.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0164","q":"Goitre is caused by the deficiency of which element in the diet?","o":["Iodine","Iron","Calcium","Zinc"],"a":0,"x":"Iodine is needed to make thyroxine. Without enough of it the thyroid gland enlarges, which is goitre. Iodised salt is the common remedy.","e":"Iodine for the thyroid; iron for haemoglobin.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0165","q":"Which vitamin is needed for the normal clotting of blood?","o":["Vitamin K","Vitamin C","Vitamin B12","Vitamin E"],"a":0,"x":"Vitamin K is needed to make several clotting factors in the liver. Its deficiency leads to prolonged bleeding.","e":"K for koagulation — the German spelling that gave the vitamin its letter.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"plants","n":"Plants","qs":[{"id":"p0166","q":"The green pigment in plants that captures sunlight for photosynthesis is called what?","o":["Chlorophyll","Carotene","Xanthophyll","Anthocyanin"],"a":0,"x":"Chlorophyll, held in the chloroplasts, absorbs sunlight and uses it to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose, releasing oxygen.","e":"Chlorophyll is green because it absorbs red and blue light and reflects green.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0167","q":"Which tissue carries water from the roots to the leaves of a plant?","o":["Xylem","Phloem","Cambium","Epidermis"],"a":0,"x":"Xylem conducts water and dissolved minerals upwards from the roots. Phloem carries the food made in the leaves to the rest of the plant.","e":"Xylem up with water; phloem around with food.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0168","q":"Nitrogen fixation in the root nodules of leguminous plants is carried out by which bacterium?","o":["Lactobacillus","Nitrosomonas","Rhizobium","Clostridium"],"a":2,"x":"Rhizobium lives in the root nodules of pulses and other legumes and converts atmospheric nitrogen into a form the plant can use, which is why pulses enrich the soil.","e":"Rhizobium in the nodules of peas, grams and beans.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"living","n":"Classification and Animals","qs":[{"id":"p0169","q":"The system of binomial nomenclature for naming living things was given by whom?","o":["Carolus Linnaeus","Charles Darwin","Gregor Mendel","Aristotle"],"a":0,"x":"Carolus Linnaeus introduced the two-part naming system in which each species gets a genus name and a species name, both written in Latin.","e":"Two names: genus with a capital letter, species in lower case, both in italics.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0170","q":"Which is the largest mammal in the world?","o":["Blue whale","African elephant","Giraffe","Hippopotamus"],"a":0,"x":"The blue whale is the largest animal known to have lived, reaching about 30 metres and 150 tonnes. The African elephant is the largest land animal.","e":"Biggest overall: blue whale. Biggest on land: African elephant.","src":["Practice question"]}]}]},{"id":"env","n":"Environment","tab":"Environment","w":"Ecology, protected areas and biodiversity, pollution and climate.","topics":[{"id":"ecology","n":"Ecology and Sustainability","qs":[{"id":"p0171","q":"The first UN Conference on the Human Environment, which led to World Environment Day, was held in 1972 in which city?","o":["Stockholm","Rio de Janeiro","Nairobi","Paris"],"a":0,"x":"The Stockholm Conference of June 1972 was the first UN conference on the environment. It led to the creation of UNEP, and 5 June is marked as World Environment Day.","e":"Stockholm 1972 → UNEP and World Environment Day on 5 June.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0172","q":"The Earth Summit of 1992, which produced the Convention on Biological Diversity, was held in which city?","o":["Kyoto","Johannesburg","Montreal","Rio de Janeiro"],"a":3,"x":"The UN Conference on Environment and Development — the Earth Summit — was held at Rio de Janeiro in 1992. It produced Agenda 21, the UNFCCC and the Convention on Biological Diversity.","e":"Rio 1992: Agenda 21, climate convention and biodiversity convention together.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"biodiversity","n":"Parks, Forests and Biodiversity","qs":[{"id":"p0173","q":"Which was the first national park to be established in India?","o":["Kaziranga National Park","Gir National Park","Bandhavgarh National Park","Jim Corbett National Park"],"a":3,"x":"Set up in 1936 as Hailey National Park in Uttarakhand, it was renamed Jim Corbett National Park and was the first national park in India. Project Tiger was launched there in 1973.","e":"1936 Hailey → Corbett; India's first national park and first tiger reserve.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0174","q":"Kaziranga National Park is famous for which animal?","o":["Asiatic lion","Snow leopard","One-horned rhinoceros","Great Indian bustard"],"a":2,"x":"Kaziranga in Assam holds the world's largest population of the Indian one-horned rhinoceros and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","e":"Kaziranga rhino (Assam), Gir lion (Gujarat), Sundarbans tiger (West Bengal).","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0175","q":"Which was the first biosphere reserve to be set up in India?","o":["Nilgiri","Nanda Devi","Sundarbans","Gulf of Mannar"],"a":0,"x":"The Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve, spread over Tamil Nadu, Kerala and Karnataka, was notified in 1986 as India's first biosphere reserve.","e":"Nilgiri 1986 — the first of India's biosphere reserves.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0176","q":"Project Tiger was launched in India in which year?","o":["1972","1973","1980","1992"],"a":1,"x":"Project Tiger began in 1973 at Jim Corbett National Park, following the Wildlife Protection Act of 1972. Project Elephant came later, in 1992.","e":"Act 1972, Project Tiger 1973, Project Elephant 1992.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"pollution","n":"Pollution","qs":[{"id":"p0177","q":"The Montreal Protocol of 1987 deals with substances that damage what?","o":["Coral reefs","Groundwater","The ozone layer","Tropical forests"],"a":2,"x":"The Montreal Protocol controls the production and use of ozone-depleting substances such as chlorofluorocarbons. 16 September is observed as World Ozone Day.","e":"Montreal ozone, Kyoto and Paris climate, Basel hazardous waste, Ramsar wetlands.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0178","q":"Excessive growth of algae in a water body caused by nutrient enrichment is called what?","o":["Biomagnification","Eutrophication","Bioremediation","Salinisation"],"a":1,"x":"Eutrophication happens when fertiliser run-off and sewage load a lake with nitrates and phosphates. The resulting algal bloom uses up dissolved oxygen and fish die.","e":"Too many nutrients, too much algae, too little oxygen.","src":["Practice question"]},{"id":"p0179","q":"A high Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) in a sample of water indicates what?","o":["The water is safe to drink","The water has a high mineral content","The water is acidic","The water is highly polluted with organic matter"],"a":3,"x":"BOD measures the oxygen that micro-organisms need to break down the organic matter in water. The more organic pollution there is, the higher the BOD.","e":"High BOD, dirty water; clean water has a low BOD.","src":["Practice question"]}]},{"id":"climate","n":"Climate Change and Treaties","qs":[{"id":"p0180","q":"The Ramsar Convention of 1971 deals with the conservation of what?","o":["Deserts","Wetlands","Mountains","Mangrove timber"],"a":1,"x":"The Ramsar Convention, signed in Ramsar, Iran, in 1971, is the international treaty for the conservation and wise use of wetlands. 2 February is World Wetlands Day.","e":"Ramsar = wetlands; Chilika was India's first Ramsar site.","src":["Practice question"]}]}]}];
